// A world saved to a file and read back: all the program's entities,
// buffers and uniques as they are, so that it goes on from a loaded
// world exactly as it would have from the one that was saved.
//
//   import world
//   system choose() { Saved.file = "game.sav" }
//   schedule slots() {
//     choose()
//     save_world() run_if Slot.save
//     load_world() run_if Slot.load
//   }
//
// `save_world` and `load_world` are steps of a schedule: between two
// systems the world is whole. `Saved.ok` says whether it went well; a
// world that could not be read is left as it was.
//
// What is declared `transient` is not saved, and a world that is loaded
// into keeps it as it has it: what belongs to the machine and not to
// the game (a window, the keys that are down), what is read from files
// anyway, what is only for a frame.
//
//   transient unique Window { .. }
//   transient buffer Pixels { color: i32 } capacity 64000
//
// Of columns and buffers only the rows in use are written. A file is
// read only by the program that wrote it, as it was built then: after a
// change to what the program declares, its old files are refused.
//
// A program with a text of any length in a component or a unique that is
// not transient cannot be saved yet (such a text is kept apart from the
// world); texts of a given length (`text[32]`) can.
//
// Implemented in devices/world.c, with what the compiler writes into the
// world's header for C (ent_world_save, ent_world_load).

// The file to write or read (where the program was started, or a path),
// and how the last `save_world` or `load_world` went.
transient unique Saved { file: text[126], ok: bool }

extern system save_world()
extern system load_world()
